WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.213
An LLM threat model should not be a one-time document.

2
00:00:03.213 --> 00:00:11.315
AI applications change as models, prompts, tools, documents, workflows, integrations, and user behavior change.

3
00:00:11.315 --> 00:00:14.089
New capabilities can create new risks.

4
00:00:14.089 --> 00:00:17.072
New data sources can create new trust boundaries.

5
00:00:17.072 --> 00:00:19.537
A model change can affect output behavior.

6
00:00:19.537 --> 00:00:21.722
A new tool can expand agency.

7
00:00:21.722 --> 00:00:26.202
A new retrieval collection can introduce new content integrity concerns.

8
00:00:26.202 --> 00:00:29.495
Incidents and near misses should feed back into the threat model.

9
00:00:29.495 --> 00:00:36.480
Teams should update diagrams, assets, abuse cases, controls, tests, and monitoring as the system evolves.

10
00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:41.758
A living threat model helps organizations avoid treating AI security as a launch checklist.

11
00:00:41.758 --> 00:00:52.065
It turns AI security into an ongoing engineering discipline supported by architecture, controls, testing, monitoring, incident learning, and regular review.
